  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n500H ridge centered over southeastern CONUS continues to linger, resulting\nin mass subsidence and hot conditions. Fresh guidance continues to \npaint heat indices around 100F across the entire region for Thursday.\nBy Friday, the ridge will be dampened a bit by a shortwave moving \nthrough eastern Canada. This acts to push the hottest conditions \nfarther south with heat indices around 100F expected again for areas \nprimarily south of the I-70 corridor. \n\nThere is some question as to how afternoon thunderstorm development\nand cloud cover may impact heat both days (see more below). \n\nBy Saturday, deep troughing moves into the Atlantic seaboard,\nresulting in deep northwesterly flow across the Ohio Valley region.\nAbove average temperatures will linger on Saturday (highs in the mid\n80s along and north of I-70, low 90s for areas south) but we'll\nfinally fall below Heat headline criteria. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nAn active pattern continues around the periphery of the ridge on\nThursday, with the Great Lakes region receiving the bulk of the MCS\ntrain of activity. However, there is the potential for portions of \ncentral Ohio to be impacted by these rounds of storms throughout the \nday on Thursday. The thunderstorm threat will then expand\nsouthwestward Thursday night into Friday as the ridge breaks down. \n\nThe bulk of the deep layer shear will remain well northeast of our\narea, however, some ripples of 25-35 knots of bulk shear will creep\ninto central Ohio by Thursday afternoon as a shortwave moves through\nthe northwesterly flow. This, combined with 2500 J/kg of SBCAPE,\nPWATs around 150% of normals, and steep low level lapse rates\nintroduces a strong/severe thunderstorm threat into our northeastern\ncounties (central Ohio) Thursday afternoon and evening hours.\nHowever, with that being said, confidence in thunderstorms making it\ndown to our area isn't overly robust. Highest confidence in\nthunderstorm development and MCS track(s) remains across northern \nand northeastern Ohio during Thursday day timeframe, given the\nslightly more westerly 0-3km shear vector. \n\nAs we head into the overnight hours, the ridge continues to dampen,\nintroducing a shortwave disturbance and stronger northwesterly flow \nbegins to encroach into our region. This shift puts us moreso into \nMCS territory in teams of shear and driving winds. However, as we \nhead into the overnight, we lose our SB instability. Despite this, an\naxis of around 1500-2000 J/kg of MUCAPE will linger overnight and \nPWATs increase to around 180% of normals. Unfortunately, trying to \ntime out MCS arrival is notoriously difficult and often depends \nlargely on previous MCS development/decay and residual outflow \nboundaries. There is some consensus between hires guidance that some\ntype of MCS remnant will move down into the I-70 corridor during the\nearly morning hours on Friday. However, how much this holds together\nremains to be seen. \n\nSimilar concerns remain in place as we head into the weekend, with a\ndeep trough digging into the Atlantic seaboard, placing our forecast\narea in deep northwesterly flow for Saturday. Showers and\nthunderstorms are again possible, though this will largely depend on\nprevious thunderstorm development and where residual cloud cover lays\nout.",
